cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Privacy-KB: a citation-anchored, date-aware model of US federal and New York privacy law"
message: "If you use this software or corpus, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Islam
    given-names: Muhammad Rakibul
    email: rakib.islam@rutgers.edu
    affiliation: Rutgers University
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-09-10
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/rakib-nyc/privacy-kb"
url: "https://rakib-nyc.github.io/privacy-kb/"
keywords:
  - privacy law
  - legal informatics
  - computable law
  - breach notification
  - HIPAA
  - provenance
  - Model Context Protocol
abstract: >-
  Privacy-KB models United States federal, New York State and New York City privacy law as
  248 provenance-anchored records over 54 instruments, and answers applicability questions
  deterministically as of a given date. Every provision is quoted verbatim from a primary
  source and anchored to the sha256 of the bytes it was cut from, so any quotation can be
  re-verified without trusting the tool. Forty-five CI gates enforce the properties the
  corpus claims: that a span appears in its source, that an effective date is derived from
  evidence rather than asserted, that a version chain has no gaps, and that a record may
  not claim a date earlier than the text it quotes.

  The engine is deterministic and contains no language model. It reports what it cannot
  determine as prominently as what it finds — coverage gaps, unverified records suppressed
  from every answer, facts never supplied, and legal characterisations the user has not
  made. A single incident may satisfy several statutory definitions at once, so an
  unasserted characterisation is returned as a decision awaiting the reader rather than
  silently excluded.

  This is a research prototype. It is not legal advice and carries no warranty. Parts of
  the corpus were assembled with AI assistance; the verification apparatus exists because
  that is true, and the limitations are published alongside the work.
